\begin{abstract}
This paper studies admissions with one open category and several reserve categories when students may qualify for more than one reserve category at once. Under the one-to-one counting rule an admitted student is counted toward at most one category, so a mechanism must decide not only who is admitted but also which category each overlapping student fills. We additionally impose a score constraint bounding the gap between the open-category cutoff and each reserve-category cutoff.

We formalize an overlapping-category model in which all reserve categories use the same score bonus. Non-wastefulness has to be restated relative to the score constraint; within-category fairness does not, because an upward within-category replacement can never destroy score-feasibility. We study a student-proposing score-elevated mechanism (SPSE) and show that it is feasible, non-wasteful, strategy-proof and free of justified envy, but not score-feasible. Score-elevated policies are known to fail cutoff rules already without overlap; what overlap adds is a distinct cause of failure, namely the category to which an overlapping student is routed. We then show that score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness are jointly unattainable \emph{even when every reserve category could be filled score-feasibly in isolation}; the obstruction is a failure of Hall's condition on the induced feasibility graph and is therefore genuinely one of overlap. Finally we describe a preference-aware overlapping de-reservation mechanism (PA-ODR) that treats score-feasibility as a hard constraint and reallocates unfulfillable reserve capacity to the open category. PA-ODR restores score-feasibility and retains within-category fairness unweakened, but forfeits strategy-proofness and freedom from justified envy, and the amount it de-reserves is order-dependent and not minimal. Minimum de-reservation is decidable by enumeration in time polynomial for a bounded number of reserve categories, leaving the general case open.

\keywords{Matching \and Affirmative action \and Reserve categories \and Overlapping types \and De-reservation \and School choice}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Reserve-based admissions and controlled school choice have been studied extensively in the matching literature \cite{Abdu05a,EHYY14a,HYY13a,EcYe15a,AyTu20a,AyTu23a}. In the most standard formulations, each student either belongs to a single protected category or to none. In many real admissions systems, however, protected categories overlap: a student may be simultaneously eligible for several reserve categories. S\"onmez and Yenmez study overlapping reserves \cite{SoYe20a} and the vertical--horizontal reservation system of Indian affirmative action, in which the two dimensions interact \cite{SoYe22a}. Related overlapping-type environments are studied by Kurata et al.\ in controlled school choice with overlapping types \cite{KHIY17a}, by Ayg\"un and B\'o in multidimensional affirmative action \cite{AyBo21a}, by Delacr\'etaz et al.\ in refugee resettlement with multidimensional matching \cite{DKT23a}, and more broadly by Goto et al.\ \cite{GKK+17a} and Kamada and Kojima \cite{KaKo24a} in matching under distributional and diversity constraints. Indian engineering admissions, the empirical setting closest to ours, are described by Baswana et al.\ \cite{BCC+19b}.

The question of \emph{which} slot or category an admitted student is counted against is itself a design variable, made explicit by Kominers and S\"onmez in the slot-specific priority framework \cite{KoSo16a}; the consequences of getting the accounting wrong are documented by Dur et al.\ \cite{DKPS18a}. Our one-to-one counting rule can be read as a slot-specific structure in which each reserve slot accepts only students eligible for its own category, with the additional twist that admissibility depends on an endogenous cutoff.

Once categories overlap, the admissions problem changes in a fundamental way. A mechanism must decide not only which students are admitted, but also how overlapping students are counted. Under one-to-one counting, an overlapping student may fill at most one reserve slot. Therefore the category assigned to a student is no longer a bookkeeping detail; it becomes an integral part of the allocation itself.

The model adds a second difficulty by imposing a score constraint. The open-category cutoff and each reserve-category cutoff may not be too far apart. This is not a stylized device: in \emph{Ashoka Kumar Thakur vs.\ Union of India} the Supreme Court of India recommended a maximum ten-point difference between the open-category and the OBC cutoff scores, and the consequences of that directive are analyzed by Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b}. It is naturally interpreted as a policy requirement that reserve admissions should not fall arbitrarily below open admissions, echoing cutoff-based concerns in affirmative-action admissions \cite{AyTu23a,ABB24a}. Under such a rule, a reserve seat may be nominally available but effectively unusable if every remaining eligible student would violate the cutoff-gap bound.

These two ingredients affect the standard desiderata of unconstrained school-choice models \cite{EHYY14a,KHIY17a,GKK+17a}, but---and this is worth stating at the outset---they affect them asymmetrically. Non-wastefulness genuinely has to be restated: a seat is not truly available unless it can be filled without violating the score rule. Within-category fairness, by contrast, does not, because replacing a matched student by an eligible student with a strictly higher adjusted score can only raise that category's cutoff, and therefore can never destroy score-feasibility. We record this observation explicitly (Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf}) so that the fairness notion used below is not mistaken for an artificially weakened one.

\subsubsection{Relation to the closest prior work.}
Two strands are especially close. S\"onmez and Yenmez study overlapping reserves \cite{SoYe20a} and the vertical--horizontal structure of Indian reservations \cite{SoYe22a}; both take the reserve capacities as given and do not impose a cutoff-gap requirement, which is the constraint that drives every negative result here. Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b} study precisely the score-elevated reserve policy and the ten-point cutoff rule, but in a setting without overlapping reserve eligibility, so the mechanism never has to decide \emph{which} reserve category a student fills.

We should be explicit about what is and is not new in the negative results, because the two strands already overlap in one respect. That a score-elevated mechanism can violate a cutoff-gap rule is \emph{not} new, and it does not require overlap: with a single reserve category, one open seat and one reserve seat, a strong open admit and a weak reserve-eligible student already produce a violation, which is the phenomenon isolated by Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b}. What overlap adds is a second, independent cause. When a student is eligible for several reserve categories and can be counted toward only one, the mechanism's routing decision itself determines whether the cutoff rule survives, and a routing that is optimal from the student's point of view can destroy a score-feasibility that a different routing would have preserved. Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} exhibits this second cause, and Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause} separates it from the first, noting in particular that the bare statement of that proposition is already available without overlap. Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} makes the same separation for the existence question: it is stated so that each reserve category is individually fillable score-feasibly, so that the obstruction cannot be attributed to a shortage of strong candidates in any one category. The de-reservation response we study is in the spirit of Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23a}.

\subsubsection{Contributions.}
We make the following contributions.

\begin{enumerate}
\item We formalize an overlapping-reserve model with a common reserve score bonus and a cutoff-gap constraint. We restate non-wastefulness relative to that constraint and show that within-category fairness needs no such restatement (Section~\ref{sec:model}).
\item We study a student-proposing score-elevated mechanism (SPSE), whose proposal--rejection structure follows the deferred-acceptance tradition of Gale and Shapley \cite{GaSh62a} and the school-choice design literature synthesized by Abdulkadiro\u{g}lu and S\"onmez \cite{AbSo03b} and Roth \cite{Roth08a}. We show it is feasible, non-wasteful, free of justified envy, and strategy-proof, but that it may violate score-feasibility. We separate two causes of that violation, only the second of which is new: the single-eligibility cause already present in Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b}, and an overlap-specific cause in which SPSE lets an overlapping student's own preference decide which reserve category she fills (Section~\ref{sec:spse}, Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause}).
\item We show that score-feasibility, open-completeness, and reserve-completeness are jointly unattainable \emph{even under the hypothesis that every reserve category is individually fillable score-feasibly}. The obstruction is thus not a shortage of strong candidates but a violation of Hall's condition on the feasibility graph induced by the open cutoff, and it disappears when no student is eligible for two categories (Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility}).
\item We propose a preference-aware overlapping de-reservation mechanism (PA-ODR) that treats the score rule as a hard constraint and de-reserves capacity that cannot be filled feasibly, in the spirit of the de-reservation policies studied by Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23a}. We prove termination, a polynomial running time after a capacity normalization (Remark~\ref{rem:paodr-time}), feasibility, score-feasibility, and non-wastefulness with respect to the final capacity vector, and we show that PA-ODR retains the \emph{unweakened} within-category fairness notion of Section~\ref{sec:model}. We then delimit the price of this: PA-ODR admits justified envy, is not strategy-proof, de-reserves an order-dependent and non-minimal amount, and can de-reserve a seat that could instead have been left idle (Section~\ref{sec:paodr}).
\item On the algorithmic side, we give a polynomial-time realization of the reserve-side selection rule as a single maximum-weight bipartite matching, and we observe that minimum de-reservation is decidable by enumeration, in time polynomial whenever the number of reserve categories is bounded; the complexity for unbounded $|T|$ is left open (Remark~\ref{rem:computation}, Proposition~\ref{prop:minderes}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Model}
\label{sec:model}

\subsection{Primitives}

We consider admissions to a single school. Let $I$ be a finite set of students, let $T$ be a finite set of reserve categories, and let $o$ denote the open category. The full set of categories is
\[
V=T\cup\{o\}.
\]

Each category $c\in V$ has capacity $q^c\in\mathbb{N}$. Each student $i\in I$ has a raw score $\sigma(i)\in\R_+$. We assume scores are distinct for simplicity. Each student also has a reserve-eligibility set
\[
\T(i)\subseteq T.
\]
We say a student $i$ has overlapping reserve categories when $|\T(i)|\ge 2$.

All reserve categories share a common bonus $k\ge 0$, and the model fixes an allowed score gap $\delta\ge 0$ that controls the difference between the cutoffs of the open and the reserve categories. Thus the model does not allow the bonus to vary by reserve category.

\begin{definition}[Application set]
For student $i$, the admissible category set is
\[
V_i:=\{o\}\cup\T(i).
\]
\end{definition}

Each student $i$ has a strict preference ordering $\succ_i$ over $V_i$. We write $r_i(c)$ for the rank of category $c$ in $\succ_i$, with rank $1$ being the best. These preferences are exogenous data taken as given by the mechanisms below; as there is a single school, students rank categories rather than schools, so $\succ_i$ records which admission route---the open category or an eligible reserve category---the student prefers. A student cannot apply to a reserve category for which she lacks eligibility; this is the only application model considered from this point onward.

\subsection{Counting Conventions and Matchings}

We distinguish two ways of counting students across categories.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{One-to-one counting:} if a student is matched, she is counted toward exactly one category chosen by the mechanism.
\item \textbf{One-to-all counting:} if a student is matched, she is counted toward every category for which she is eligible.
\end{itemize}

This paper adopts the one-to-one counting convention commonly used in the literature on controlled school choice with overlapping types, multidimensional affirmative action, and matching under diversity constraints \cite{KHIY17a,AyBo21a,GKK+17a}. The convention is motivated by admissions systems in which a student may qualify for multiple reserve categories but may be counted toward the capacity of only one category. For example, a student eligible under both a socioeconomic and a geographic criterion may be admitted through either reserve category, but her admission cannot simultaneously fill both quotas. Consequently, the mechanism must determine not only which students are admitted, but also which category each admitted student fills. We therefore represent an allocation as a matching between students and categories.

\begin{definition}[Matching]
A matching is a tuple
\[
\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}
\]
such that $\mu_c\subseteq I$ for every $c\in V$. Denote the set of all matched students by
\[
M(\mu):=\bigcup_{c\in V}\mu_c.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Feasibility]
A matching $\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}$ is feasible if:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $|\mu_c|\le q^c$ for every category $c\in V$;
\item $\mu_c\cap \mu_{c'}=\emptyset$ for every distinct pair $c,c'\in V$; and
\item $c\in V_i$ for every student $i\in \mu_c$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

The second condition is the formal statement of one-to-one counting.

\subsection{Adjusted Scores}

The open category ranks students by raw scores, while reserve categories rank eligible students after adding the common reserve bonus. Because a student may only be assigned to a category in $V_i$, it suffices to define the adjusted score on that domain.

\begin{definition}[Adjusted score]
For each student $i$ and each admissible category $c\in V_i$, define
\[
\sigma_c(i):=
\begin{cases}
\sigma(i), & \text{if } c=o,\\[4pt]
\sigma(i)+k, & \text{if } c=t\in\T(i).
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

Thus every reserve category gives the same bonus $k$ to students eligible for that category. The special case $k=0$ recovers the baseline model in which every category ranks students by raw score; all definitions and results below cover that case, so we do not treat it separately. Since the bonus is common, reserve categories share the same score ranking over their eligible students; differences across reserve categories come from eligibility, capacities, and students' category preferences, not from differences in score bonuses.

\subsection{Cutoffs and Score-Feasibility}

\begin{definition}[Cutoffs]
For a matching $\mu$, define the open-category cutoff by
\[
e_o(\mu):=\min_{i\in\mu_o}\sigma(i),
\]
with the convention $e_o(\mu)=-\infty$ if $\mu_o=\emptyset$, and, for each reserve category $t\in T$, the adjusted reserve cutoff by
\[
e_t^+(\mu):=\min_{i\in\mu_t}\sigma_t(i),
\]
with the convention $e_t^+(\mu)=+\infty$ if $\mu_t=\emptyset$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Score-feasibility]
\label{def:scorefeas}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is score-feasible if, for every reserve category $t\in T$,
\[
e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le \delta.
\]
\end{definition}

With the conventions above, we read the difference $e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)$ as $-\infty$ whenever $\mu_o=\emptyset$ or $\mu_t=\emptyset$ (including the case in which both are empty, where the expression would otherwise be the indeterminate $-\infty-(+\infty)$). The condition is therefore vacuous in those cases and no side condition is needed. The key point is that no reserve cutoff may fall too far below the open cutoff.

This definition treats the reserve-category bonus as institutionally legitimate. What matters is not the raw-score difference between open and reserve cutoffs, but the difference between the open raw cutoff and the reserve adjusted cutoff. The substantive role of the condition is to prevent reserve admissions from drifting too far below open admissions once that adjusted-score interpretation is accepted.

One might worry that this grants the reserve advantage twice. It does not: $k$ only shifts the score scale used for reserve categories, while $\delta$ constrains the realized cutoffs on that adjusted scale. In raw scores the two compound---a reserve admit may fall $\delta+k$ below the open cutoff, since score-feasibility requires only $\sigma(i)+k\ge e_o(\mu)-\delta$---but this is deliberate, since $\delta$ is meant to bound only the gap left after the sanctioned adjustment $k$. The two parameters therefore play genuinely different roles, and the boundary case $k=0$ is the one in which they coincide: there the adjusted scale is the raw scale and $\delta$ alone bounds the raw cutoff gap, which is exactly the ten-point rule studied by Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b}. Everything below covers that case, so we do not treat it separately.

At the same time, score-feasibility is conceptually distinct from the ranking rule that categories use. A mechanism may rank students within a category by adjusted scores without thereby enforcing the cutoff-gap condition. Score-feasibility should therefore be read as an ex post outcome requirement on cutoffs rather than as something implied by the within-category ranking rule. This separation is precisely why a second mechanism becomes necessary in Section~\ref{sec:paodr}.

\subsection{Desiderata}

In ordinary matching problems, wastefulness is often defined by the existence of an empty seat that an unmatched student could occupy. In the present setting, that is not enough: the student can only occupy the empty seat while preserving score-feasibility.

\begin{definition}[Non-wastefulness]
\label{def:nw}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is non-wasteful if there do not exist an unmatched student $i\in I\setminus M(\mu)$ and a category $c\in V_i$ such that adding $i$ to category $c$ yields a matching that is both feasible and score-feasible.
\end{definition}

This definition reflects the fact that a reserve seat may be empty and yet unusable, because any additional student would violate the cutoff-gap restriction. Note that non-wastefulness is stated for feasible matchings, not only for score-feasible ones; this matters because SPSE below is non-wasteful without being score-feasible. Note also that the requirement is that the \emph{enlarged} matching be score-feasible, not that score-feasibility be preserved. The distinction is immaterial for score-feasible $\mu$, but it is the right reading for score-infeasible $\mu$: the alternative would declare every score-infeasible matching vacuously non-wasteful. We use the same reading in Definition~\ref{def:nw-hat} below.

The natural within-category complaint is that a student with a lower adjusted score is counted in category $t$ while a student with a higher adjusted score who is also eligible for $t$ remains unmatched.

\begin{definition}[Within-category fairness]
\label{def:fair}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is fair within categories if there do not exist a reserve category $t\in T$ and students $i,j$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item $t\in\T(i)\cap\T(j)$,
\item $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$,
\item $j\in\mu_t$, and
\item $i\notin M(\mu)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

Because the model uses one-to-one counting, the relevant condition is that $j$ is actually assigned to category $t$, not merely eligible for $t$. The definition ranges over reserve categories only. This is deliberate: it is the minimal notion on which the two mechanisms of this paper agree, and it isolates the guarantee that a reserve seat is never occupied by a student whom a stronger eligible claimant could displace. Complaints directed at the open category, and complaints by students who are already matched, are the business of Definition~\ref{def:nje} below, which is where the two mechanisms part company.

Definition~\ref{def:fair} makes no reference to the score constraint, and one might expect that it should, by analogy with Definition~\ref{def:nw}: perhaps $i$ should be barred from complaining when installing her in place of $j$ would break score-feasibility. The following observation shows that such a weakening would have no content.

\begin{lemma}[Within-category fairness needs no score-relative weakening]
\label{lem:fair-sf}
Let $\mu$ be a feasible matching, let $t\in T$, let $j\in\mu_t$ and let $i\notin M(\mu)$ with $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$. Let $\mu'$ be obtained from $\mu$ by replacing $j$ with $i$ in $\mu_t$. Then $\mu'$ is feasible, and if $\mu$ is score-feasible then so is $\mu'$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Feasibility is immediate: $|\mu'_t|=|\mu_t|\le q^t$, the student $i$ was previously assigned to no category so no student is counted twice, and $t\in\T(i)\subseteq V_i$ gives eligibility. For score-feasibility, note that the swap changes no category other than $t$ and leaves $\mu_o$ untouched, so $e_o(\mu')=e_o(\mu)$ and $e_{t'}^+(\mu')=e_{t'}^+(\mu)$ for every $t'\neq t$. For $t$ itself, $\mu'_t=(\mu_t\setminus\{j\})\cup\{i\}$ with $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$, so
\[
e_t^+(\mu')=\min_{h\in\mu'_t}\sigma_t(h)\ \ge\ \min_{h\in\mu_t}\sigma_t(h)=e_t^+(\mu).
\]
Hence $e_o(\mu')-e_t^+(\mu')\le e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le\delta$, and every other constraint is unchanged.
\end{proof}

So an upward within-category replacement never endangers the cutoff rule, and the score-relative version of Definition~\ref{def:fair} coincides with Definition~\ref{def:fair} on score-feasible matchings. The asymmetry with non-wastefulness is genuine: adding a \emph{new} student to a reserve category can lower that category's cutoff and hence break score-feasibility, whereas \emph{replacing} a student by a better one cannot. This is why only Definition~\ref{def:nw} carries a score-feasibility clause.

Definition~\ref{def:fair} restricts attention to unmatched claimants and to reserve categories. The following stronger requirement, which is the standard no-justified-envy condition of the school-choice literature transposed to categories, also lets a \emph{matched} student complain about a category she ranks above her own, and includes the open category among the objects of complaint. We use it to separate the two mechanisms in Section~\ref{sec:paodr}.

\begin{definition}[No justified envy]
\label{def:nje}
A feasible matching $\mu$ has no justified envy if there do not exist a student $i$ and a category $c\in V_i$ such that
\begin{enumerate}
\item either $i\notin M(\mu)$, or $i\in\mu_{c'}$ for some $c'\in V_i$ with $c\succ_i c'$; and
\item either $|\mu_c|<q^c$, or there is a student $j\in\mu_c$ with $\sigma_c(j)<\sigma_c(i)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

No justified envy implies within-category fairness: if $i\notin M(\mu)$, $j\in\mu_t$ and $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$ with $t\in\T(i)$, then $c=t$ witnesses a justified envy. The converse fails, as Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy} shows.

Definition~\ref{def:nje} is stated \emph{without} a score-feasibility clause, and---unlike Definition~\ref{def:fair}---this is a genuine choice rather than a fact, because Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf} does not extend to it. The lemma covers replacements only, whereas Definition~\ref{def:nje} admits two further kinds of claim, both of which can interact with the cutoff rule. The first alternative of condition~2 lets $i$ claim a vacant seat in $c$, and adding a student to a category can lower that category's cutoff, exactly as in Definition~\ref{def:nw}. The second case of condition~1 lets a \emph{matched} student move out of $c'$, and if $c'=o$ this \emph{raises} the open cutoff and can therefore break score-feasibility for reserve categories untouched by the move. So one could state a score-relative weakening of Definition~\ref{def:nje}, in which a claim counts only if honouring it leaves a score-feasible matching, and this weakening would have content.

We nevertheless use the unweakened notion, for two reasons. On the positive side it makes Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-nje} stronger. On the negative side it makes Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy} weaker, so the burden is to check that the envy exhibited there is not one that the score rule would have excused; we verify in that example that honouring the claim does leave a score-feasible matching, so the failure survives the weakened reading as well.

\subsection{Methodological Tension}

There is a structural circularity in the model: the set of feasible reserve assignments depends on the open cutoff, while the open cutoff depends on which students are left for the open category after reserve assignments are made. Hence score-feasibility is not a static eligibility condition. This is exactly why direct greedy quota-filling is unreliable.

The two mechanisms below respond to this tension differently. SPSE keeps the original capacity vector and uses deferred-acceptance logic. PA-ODR instead treats the cutoff rule as hard and allows de-reservation, following the broad policy logic studied by Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23a}: fill the open category first, identify reserve assignments that remain feasible relative to that cutoff, and transfer any unfillable reserve capacity to the open category.

\section{Student-Proposing Score-Elevated Mechanism}
\label{sec:spse}

We first introduce a student-proposing deferred-acceptance variant adapted to category-adjusted scores, stated as Algorithm~\ref{alg:spse}.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Student-Proposing Score-Elevated Mechanism (SPSE)}
\label{alg:spse}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Student set $I$, category set $V=T\cup\{o\}$, capacities $(q^c)_{c\in V}$, student category preferences $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$, raw scores $\sigma$, eligibility sets $\T(\cdot)$, common reserve bonus $k$
\Ensure Matching $\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}$
\State Initialize $\mu_c\gets\emptyset$ for all $c\in V$
\State Mark every student unmatched
\State Set $V_i:=\{o\}\cup\T(i)$ and compute $\sigma_c(i)$ for every student $i$ and every $c\in V_i$
\While{there exists an unmatched student $i$ who still has an unapplied category in $V_i$}
    \State $i$ applies to her most preferred unapplied category $c\in V_i$
    \State Category $c$ considers $\mu_c\cup\{i\}$ and keeps at most $q^c$ students with highest $\sigma_c$
    \State Every rejected student becomes unmatched
\EndWhile
\State \Return $\mu$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

This mechanism preserves one-to-one counting because a student can be held by at most one category at a time. It differs from ordinary student-proposing deferred acceptance only in that the objects proposed to are categories rather than schools, and in the reserve-side score elevation. In particular each student applies to each category of $V_i$ at most once, so the loop executes at most $\sum_{i\in I}|V_i|\le|I|\,(|T|+1)$ times and Algorithm~\ref{alg:spse} terminates.

\subsection{Properties of SPSE}

\begin{proposition}[Feasibility]
The output of SPSE is feasible.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By construction, each category keeps at most its capacity at every step, and a student is never held by two categories simultaneously. Moreover, $i$ only ever applies to categories in $V_i$. Therefore the final output respects capacity constraints, one-to-one counting, and eligibility.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Non-wastefulness]
The output of SPSE is non-wasteful in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:nw}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Suppose an unmatched student $i$ could be added to some admissible category $c\in V_i$ so that the result is feasible and score-feasible. Feasibility of the enlarged matching requires $|\mu_c|<q^c$. Since $i$ is unmatched at termination, the loop guard implies she has applied to every category in $V_i$, including $c$, and was rejected by $c$. But a category rejects a student only when it is full, and once full it never releases capacity: it only ever swaps a held student for a strictly better applicant. Hence $|\mu_c|=q^c$ in the final matching, contradicting $|\mu_c|<q^c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[No justified envy]
\label{prop:spse-nje}
The output of SPSE has no justified envy in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:nje}. Consequently it is fair within categories in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:fair}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mu$ be the output and suppose $i$ and $c\in V_i$ satisfy condition~1 of Definition~\ref{def:nje}. In either case of that condition, $c$ is strictly better for $i$ than her final assignment, so during the run $i$ applied to $c$ and was subsequently rejected by it: a student applies to the categories of $V_i$ in the order of $\succ_i$, and she is held by a category only until it rejects her, so she can end unmatched or held by a strictly worse category only after $c$ has rejected her.

Consider the moment of that rejection. A category rejects an applicant only when it already holds $q^c$ students, and it rejects the lowest-$\sigma_c$ member of $\mu_c\cup\{i\}$; since scores are distinct, every student it retains has adjusted score strictly greater than $\sigma_c(i)$. Thereafter $c$ never drops below $q^c$ held students and only ever replaces a held student by a strictly better applicant, so at termination $|\mu_c|=q^c$ and $\sigma_c(j)>\sigma_c(i)$ for every $j\in\mu_c$. Both alternatives in condition~2 therefore fail, and no justified envy exists. The second statement follows from the implication recorded after Definition~\ref{def:nje}.
\end{proof}

Since a student's raw score and eligibility set are verifiable data, the only object she can misreport is her category preference $\succ_i$. Under that reading SPSE inherits the incentive properties of deferred acceptance.

\begin{proposition}[Strategy-proofness]
\label{prop:spse-sp}
SPSE is strategy-proof: no student can obtain a strictly $\succ_i$-better assignment by reporting a preference ordering over $V_i$ other than $\succ_i$, holding the reports of the other students fixed.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix the primitives $\sigma$, $\T(\cdot)$, $k$ and $(q^c)_{c\in V}$. Treat each category $c\in V$ as a college with capacity $q^c$ whose priority order over its acceptable students---those $i$ with $c\in V_i$---is the strict ranking induced by $\sigma_c$, which is well defined because raw scores are distinct. These priorities are responsive to $q^c$ by construction, since a category always retains the $q^c$ highest-$\sigma_c$ applicants available to it. Each student $i$ has a strict preference ordering over $V_i$, all of whose elements are acceptable to her. The resulting economy is therefore an instance of the college-admissions model of Gale and Shapley \cite{GaSh62a}, and Algorithm~\ref{alg:spse} is exactly the student-proposing deferred-acceptance algorithm on that instance. Strategy-proofness for the proposing side is then the theorem of Dubins and Freedman \cite{DuFr81a} and Roth \cite{Roth82a}.
\end{proof}

We now turn to the negative side. The next example shows that SPSE can violate score-feasibility, and that the failure is caused specifically by the category to which the overlapping student is assigned.

\begin{proposition}[SPSE does not guarantee score-feasibility]
\label{prop:spse-not-sf}
The output of SPSE is not necessarily score-feasible, even when a matching that is simultaneously score-feasible, non-wasteful and fair within categories exists.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$ with
\[
q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1,\qquad k=5,\qquad \delta=10,
\]
and consider three students
\[
i_1(100,\emptyset),\qquad i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\}),\qquad i_3(82,\{t_2\}),
\]
with preferences $t_1\succ_{i_2} t_2\succ_{i_2} o$ and $t_2\succ_{i_3} o$. The adjusted scores are $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100$ and $\sigma_{t_2}(i_3)=87$.

In round 1, $i_1$ applies to $o$, $i_2$ applies to $t_1$, and $i_3$ applies to $t_2$. Each category receives exactly one applicant and holds her, so SPSE terminates with
\[
\mu_o=\{i_1\},\qquad \mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\},\qquad \mu_{t_2}=\{i_3\}.
\]
Then $e_o(\mu)=100$ and $e_{t_2}^+(\mu)=87$, so
\[
e_o(\mu)-e_{t_2}^+(\mu)=13>10=\delta,
\]
and the output violates score-feasibility.

Now consider instead the matching $\nu$ with $\nu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\nu_{t_2}=\{i_2\}$, and $\nu_{t_1}=\emptyset$. Here $e_o(\nu)=100$ and $e_{t_2}^+(\nu)=100$, so the gap is $0$, and $\nu_{t_1}=\emptyset$ makes the constraint for $t_1$ vacuous; hence $\nu$ is score-feasible. It is non-wasteful: the only student unmatched under $\nu$ is $i_3$, whose application set is $\{t_2,o\}$, and both $t_2$ and $o$ are at capacity. It is also fair within categories: the only unmatched student is $i_3$, the only reserve category she is eligible for is $t_2$, and its sole member $i_2$ satisfies $\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100>87=\sigma_{t_2}(i_3)$. Thus a matching with all three properties exists, but SPSE does not find it.
\end{proof}

Two comments on this proof are in order, one about what the alternative matching $\nu$ costs and one about what the example is evidence for.

First, $\nu$ is not better than the SPSE outcome in every respect, and we do not claim that it is. SPSE matches all three students whereas $\nu$ matches only two, and $\nu$ has justified envy in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:nje}, since $i_2$ is assigned to $t_2$ while she prefers $t_1$ and $|\nu_{t_1}|=0<q^{t_1}$. What Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} establishes is therefore not that $\nu$ dominates, but that the three properties in its statement do not by themselves force a score-feasible outcome, so that score-feasibility is an independent requirement which has to be bought---here at the price of a seat. This is the same trade-off that Section~\ref{sec:paodr} pays in the currency of reserve capacity.

Second, and more importantly, one should not read the example as showing that score-elevated mechanisms fail the cutoff rule only under overlap. They do not, and the following remark separates the two causes.

\begin{nremark}[Two causes of score-infeasibility, one of them new]
\label{rem:overlap-cause}
Score-elevated reserve policies can violate a cutoff-gap rule with no overlap whatsoever, and this is precisely the inconsistency identified by Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b}. The minimal witness has a single reserve category. Take $T=\{t_1\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, and students $i_1(100,\emptyset)$ and $i_2(80,\{t_1\})$ with $t_1\succ_{i_2}o$. SPSE returns $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, and $e_o(\mu)-e_{t_1}^+(\mu)=100-85=15>\delta$. Here the cause is simply that the only eligible candidate is too weak, and no assignment decision is at fault; indeed the matching leaving $t_1$ empty is score-feasible, non-wasteful and fair within categories, so even the exact statement of Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} already holds without overlap.

What overlap contributes is a second and logically independent cause. In the instance of Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} both $t_1$ and $t_2$ are acceptable homes for $i_2$ and her adjusted score is the same in either, so no candidate is too weak for the seat she ends up justifying: $i_2$ alone would satisfy the cutoff rule in whichever category she filled. SPSE resolves the choice by $i_2$'s own preference, which sends her to $t_1$ and leaves $t_2$ to the much weaker $i_3$. Under one-to-one counting it is that routing decision, and not the quality of the applicant pool, that breaks the cutoff rule; the same pool admits a score-feasible routing. This cause cannot arise when no student is eligible for more than one reserve category, because then there is nothing to route, and it is therefore invisible in the single-eligibility setting of \cite{AyTu23b}. The remainder of the paper is concerned with this second cause, and Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} is stated so as to exclude the first.
\end{nremark}

\subsection{An Impossibility Driven by Overlap}

The previous proposition shows that SPSE misses a score-feasible matching that does exist. We now show that sometimes no such matching exists at all, once one insists on using all capacity.

As Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause} warns, an impossibility of this kind is easy to obtain for the uninteresting reason that some category has no sufficiently strong eligible student: in the two-student instance of that remark, open-completeness forces $e_o=100$, no eligible student clears the resulting bar, and reserve-completeness already fails on its own. To exclude that reason we build its negation into the hypothesis.

\begin{definition}[Individual completability]
\label{def:indcomp}
An instance is \emph{individually completable} if for every reserve category $t\in T$ there is a feasible matching $\mu$ that is score-feasible, satisfies open-completeness, and has $|\mu_t|=q^t$.
\end{definition}

Thus in an individually completable instance every announced reserve quota can be honoured in full, score-feasibly and without disturbing the open category---provided one is allowed to ignore the other reserve categories while doing so. The impossibility is that these separately keepable promises cannot be kept together.

\begin{proposition}[Impossibility under full capacity utilization]
\label{prop:impossibility}
There is an individually completable instance (Definition~\ref{def:indcomp}) that admits no feasible matching simultaneously satisfying score-feasibility, \emph{open-completeness}, and \emph{reserve-completeness}, where open-completeness requires that $\mu_o$ consist of the $q^o$ highest raw-score students in $I$, and reserve-completeness requires that $|\mu_t|=q^t$ for every reserve category $t$ for which at least $q^t$ students are eligible.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$ with
\[
q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1,\qquad k=5,\qquad \delta=10,
\]
and consider four students
\[
i_1(100,\emptyset),\quad i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\}),\quad i_3(80,\{t_1\}),\quad i_4(78,\{t_2\}).
\]

Open-completeness forces $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, since $i_1$ is the unique highest raw-score student and $q^o=1$. Hence $e_o(\mu)=100$, and score-feasibility requires every student assigned to a reserve category $t$ to satisfy $\sigma_t(i)\ge e_o(\mu)-\delta=90$. Checking the three remaining students:
\[
\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100\ge 90,\qquad
\sigma_{t_1}(i_3)=85<90,\qquad
\sigma_{t_2}(i_4)=83<90 .
\]
So $i_2$ is the only student who may be placed in any reserve category at all.

The instance is individually completable. For $t_1$, take $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$ and $\mu_{t_2}=\emptyset$: it is open-complete, $|\mu_{t_1}|=1=q^{t_1}$, and $e_o(\mu)-e_{t_1}^+(\mu)=100-100=0\le\delta$ while the constraint for the empty $t_2$ is vacuous. Exchanging the roles of $t_1$ and $t_2$ does the same for $t_2$. So each quota is separately honourable in full.

Nevertheless both quotas cannot be honoured at once. Both $t_1$ and $t_2$ have at least $q^t=1$ eligible student, namely $\{i_2,i_3\}$ and $\{i_2,i_4\}$ respectively, so reserve-completeness demands $|\mu_{t_1}|=|\mu_{t_2}|=1$. That requires two distinct reserve placements, but by one-to-one counting $i_2$ may be counted toward at most one category. Hence some reserve category must admit $i_3$ or $i_4$, violating score-feasibility. Therefore no feasible matching satisfies all three requirements.
\end{proof}

A word on the two completeness axioms, since their strength is what makes the statement possible. Both are meant to be read as institutional commitments rather than as normative optima. Open-completeness says that the open category is genuinely open---it is filled by raw merit from the whole applicant pool, with no student held back because she would be more useful elsewhere---which is the defining feature of an open category in reserve systems. Reserve-completeness says that an announced quota must be honoured whenever the eligible population is large enough to honour it, which is how quotas are stated in policy documents: eligibility is counted category by category, precisely because the policy is written without reference to overlap. The proposition should therefore be read as saying that these two commitments, each unobjectionable in a world without overlap, become jointly incompatible with the cutoff rule once overlap is present. Weakening reserve-completeness to demand only what is \emph{jointly} fillable would of course restore consistency, but it would also concede the point at issue, namely that overlap silently reduces what a stated quota can deliver. It is exactly this concession that PA-ODR makes explicit, in the form of de-reservation.

\begin{nremark}[The obstruction is overlap, not weak candidates]
\label{rem:hall}
Individual completability is exactly what rules out the familiar observation that a category may have no sufficiently strong eligible student, and it is worth naming the combinatorial fact that takes its place. In the language of Section~\ref{sec:paodr}, let $G(e)$ be the bipartite feasibility graph induced by the open cutoff $e=100$, and write $N(S)$ for the set of students adjacent in $G(e)$ to some category in $S\subseteq T$. Then $N(\{t_1,t_2\})=\{i_2\}$, so
\[
\bigl|N(\{t_1,t_2\})\bigr| = 1 < 2 = q^{t_1}+q^{t_2},
\]
and the capacitated form of Hall's condition---that $|N(S)|\ge\sum_{t\in S}q^{t}$ for every $S\subseteq T$---fails. The two hypotheses now sit in a clean relation: individual completability is Hall's condition restricted to singletons $S=\{t\}$, and the impossibility is that this does not imply the condition for larger $S$. Since the two coincide when the neighbourhoods $N(\{t\})$ are pairwise disjoint, the gap between them is exactly overlap, and the impossibility disappears entirely if no student is eligible for two categories at once. This also explains why the mechanism of the next section is built around a bipartite matching rather than around category-by-category quota filling.
\end{nremark}

The point is that once the score-gap rule binds, the open and reserve capacities cannot both be fully utilized: some seat must be left idle or reallocated. This is precisely the room that the de-reservation of the next section exploits.

\section{Preference-Aware Overlapping De-Reservation}
\label{sec:paodr}

We now turn to a mechanism, stated as Algorithm~\ref{alg:paodr}, that treats score-feasibility as a hard constraint and resolves overlapping eligibility using student category preferences.

\subsection{The Feasibility Graph and Preference-Aware Lexicographic Matching}

For any fixed open cutoff $e$, we construct a bipartite graph $G(e)$ whose left side is the set of students not assigned to the open category and whose right side is the set of reserve categories. An edge $(i,t)$ exists if and only if
\[
t\in\T(i)\quad\text{and}\quad \sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta.
\]

Thus reserve admissibility is determined by the common adjusted score together with reserve eligibility. With a common bonus, a student who qualifies for several reserve categories is either score-admissible for all of those categories at the cutoff $e$ or for none of them. The graph is still needed because one-to-one counting requires the mechanism to decide which one of several eligible reserve categories an overlapping student should fill.

Write $P(e)$ for the set of edges of $G(e)$, the \emph{admissible pairs}, and fix once and for all an injective index $p:P(e)\to\{1,\dots,|P(e)|\}$, obtained for instance from a fixed linear order on $I$ and on $T$. Among all capacitated matchings on $G(e)$, define \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} as the one chosen by the following priority order:
\begin{enumerate}
\item maximize the number of matched pairs;
\item among those, lexicographically maximize the weakly decreasingly sorted vector of matched elevated scores $\sigma_t(i)=\sigma(i)+k$;
\item among those, lexicographically minimize the weakly decreasingly sorted vector of preference ranks $r_i(t)$ of the matched pairs;
\item among those, minimize $\sum_{(i,t)\in\mu}2^{\,p(i,t)}$.
\end{enumerate}

Criterion 4 is a pure tie-breaker with no normative content, and it is needed: criteria 1--3 do not in general single out a matching. If two students are eligible for the same two unit-capacity categories and rank them in the same order, the two ways of assigning them have identical cardinality, identical score vectors and identical rank vectors. Since distinct sets of admissible pairs have distinct sums of distinct powers of two, criterion 4 always resolves such ties, so \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is well defined.

Criterion 3 compares matchings by their worst preference rank first, then by their second-worst, and so on, so it is an egalitarian tie-breaker. With a common bonus, the same student has the same elevated score in every reserve category for which she is eligible. The division of labour between criteria 2 and 3 is in fact sharper than this suggests: the first two criteria already pin down \emph{who} is admitted on the reserve side, leaving criteria 3 and 4 to decide only \emph{where}.

\begin{lemma}[Criteria 1 and 2 determine the admitted set]
\label{lem:matroid}
Fix an open cutoff $e$ and capacities $(\hat q^{t})_{t\in T}$ for the right side of $G(e)$. All capacitated matchings on $G(e)$ satisfying criteria 1 and 2 match exactly the same set of students. Consequently, criteria 3 and 4 only choose the assignment of that set to reserve categories.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Replace each category $t$ by $\hat q^{t}$ parallel copies, so that a capacitated matching on $G(e)$ becomes an ordinary matching in a bipartite graph whose right side is the set of copies. The collection of student sets that can be matched in a bipartite graph is exactly the family of independent sets of the transversal matroid $\mathcal{M}$ induced by that graph, and criterion 1 restricts attention to its bases. Assign to each student $i$ the weight $\sigma(i)+k$; because raw scores are distinct, these weights are pairwise distinct. Criterion 2 selects the bases of $\mathcal{M}$ whose weight vector, sorted decreasingly, is lexicographically maximal. For a matroid with distinct weights this is precisely the output of the greedy algorithm, which scans the students in decreasing weight and keeps $i$ whenever the set kept so far remains independent. The greedy run is deterministic, so the resulting basis is unique.
\end{proof}

Thus criterion 2 determines which score profile is admitted on the reserve side, while criterion 3 is the device for distributing overlapping students across reserve categories once that profile is fixed. Overall, the rule ensures that category preferences matter only after size and score performance have already been optimized. The matroid structure exploited in the proof is the same one that underlies Fleiner's fixed-point treatment of stable matching \cite{Flei03a} and the substitutable choice functions of the constrained-matching literature \cite{GKK+17a,KoSo16a}; what is specific here is that the ground set is defined by an endogenous cutoff.

\begin{nremark}[Computation]
\label{rem:computation}
\textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is computable in polynomial time. Since the common bonus makes $\sigma_t(i)=\sigma(i)+k$ independent of $t$, write $s(i)$ for the rank of $\sigma(i)+k$ in increasing order among the distinct elevated scores of students incident to $P(e)$, put $n:=|P(e)|\le|I|\,|T|$ and $B:=n+1$, and assign to each admissible pair $(i,t)$ the integer weight
\[
w(i,t) := C_1 + C_2\cdot 2^{\,s(i)} - C_3\cdot B^{\,r_i(t)} - 2^{\,p(i,t)},
\]
where $C_3>\sum_{(i,t)\in P(e)}2^{p(i,t)}$, $C_2>\sum_{(i,t)\in P(e)}\bigl(C_3 B^{r_i(t)}+2^{p(i,t)}\bigr)$, and $C_1>\sum_{(i,t)\in P(e)}\bigl|C_2 2^{s(i)}-C_3B^{r_i(t)}-2^{p(i,t)}\bigr|$. A maximum-weight capacitated bipartite matching under $w$ realizes the four criteria in order.

The $C_1$ term dominates every other contribution and therefore maximizes cardinality. This step deserves one line of care, since a naive comparison of a matching of size $m+1$ with one of size $m$ would need $C_1$ to exceed \emph{twice} the total variation of the remaining terms. It does not, because those terms are individually positive: for any admissible pair, $s(i)\ge1$ gives $C_2 2^{s(i)}\ge 2C_2>C_3B^{r_i(t)}+2^{p(i,t)}$ by the choice of $C_2$. Writing $R(\mu)$ for the sum of the non-$C_1$ contributions of $\mu$, we therefore have $0\le R(\mu)<C_1$ for every matching, whence a matching of size $m+1$ beats one of size $m$ by $C_1+R(\mu)-R(\mu')\ge C_1-R(\mu')>0$. Among maximum-cardinality matchings the $C_2$ term dominates the rest, and comparing $\sum 2^{s(i)}$ as a binary number is exactly the lexicographic comparison of the decreasingly sorted score vector: no carrying can occur, because a matching uses each student at most once and raw scores are distinct, so the exponents $s(i)$ occurring in a given matching are pairwise distinct. This last point is why base $2$ is safe for the score term but \emph{not} for the rank term. Preference ranks are shared across students, so distinct matched pairs may carry the same exponent $r_i(t)$, and $\sum 2^{r_i(t)}$ would then carry and stop tracking the sorted rank vector; for instance the rank multisets $\{3,1,1\}$ and $\{2,2,2\}$ both sum to $12$, although the second is strictly better under criterion 3. Taking the base $B$ to exceed the number of admissible pairs, hence the size of any matching, makes the base-$B$ digits of $\sum B^{r_i(t)}$ equal to the number of matched pairs of each rank, so that comparing these integers is exactly the lexicographic comparison of the decreasingly sorted rank vectors. Finally the $-2^{p}$ term implements criterion 4, whose exponents are distinct by construction.

The largest weight is bounded by a product of $2^{|I|+1}$, $B^{|T|+2}$ and $2^{n+1}$, so the weights require $O(|I|\,|T|)$ bits; with integer arithmetic of that width the computation runs in time polynomial in the input size, e.g.\ via min-cost flow. The matching subproblem is thus a standard capacitated assignment problem over a feasibility graph, and it fits within the general distributional-constraint framework studied by Goto et al.\ \cite{GKK+17a}.

We state the single-weight version because it certifies polynomiality in one step, but it is a heavy instrument and an implementation need not use it. By Lemma~\ref{lem:matroid} the set admitted on the reserve side is already determined by criteria 1 and 2, and can be computed by the matroid greedy algorithm: scan students in decreasing $\sigma(i)+k$ and keep each one whose addition still leaves the kept set matchable. Fixing that set, criteria 3 and 4 only choose an assignment of it to categories, which is an ordinary min-cost capacitated assignment with the small integer costs $C_3B^{r_i(t)}+2^{p(i,t)}$. This two-phase version is the practical one; the weights above merely show that no phase structure is required.
\end{nremark}

\subsection{PA-ODR Algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Preference-Aware Overlapping De-Reservation (PA-ODR)}
\label{alg:paodr}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Student set $I$, raw scores $\sigma$, eligibility sets $\T(\cdot)$, category preferences $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$, capacities $q^o,(q^t)_{t\in T}$, common reserve bonus $k$, score-gap parameter $\delta$, a fixed linear order $\prec$ on $T$
\Ensure Matching $\mu=(\mu_o,(\mu_t)_{t\in T})$
\State Initialize effective capacities $\hat q^o\gets q^o$ and $\hat q^t\gets q^t$ for all $t\in T$
\Loop
    \State Assign the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ raw-score students to the open category; call this set $\mu_o$
    \State Let $e=\min_{i\in\mu_o}\sigma(i)$ if $\mu_o\neq\emptyset$, and $e=-\infty$ otherwise
    \State Construct the bipartite graph $G(e)$ with edge $(i,t)$ iff $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$
    \State Give category $t$ capacity $\hat q^t$ for each $t\in T$
    \State Compute the reserve assignment $(\mu_t)_{t\in T}$ using \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}
    \If{there exists $t$ with $|\mu_t|<\hat q^t$}
        \State Let $t^\ast$ be the $\prec$-first such category
        \State Update $\hat q^{t^\ast}\gets\hat q^{t^\ast}-1$ and $\hat q^o\gets\hat q^o+1$
    \Else
        \State \Return $\mu=(\mu_o,(\mu_t)_{t\in T})$
    \EndIf
\EndLoop
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

The order $\prec$ is part of the mechanism's specification and makes the output well defined; Section~\ref{sec:order} discusses what is at stake in choosing it.

\subsection{Interpretation}

PA-ODR treats score-feasibility as a hard institutional constraint. The open category is always filled first by the highest raw scores. Reserve assignment is then computed only among student-category pairs that remain feasible relative to the induced open cutoff. If some reserve category cannot fill its effective capacity, part of that capacity is de-reserved and transferred to the open category.

This gives the mechanism a very clear policy meaning. It preserves merit ordering in the open category, uses the common elevated score in reserve categories, and uses preferences only to break ties among already-feasible reserve placements. But it may change the effective reserve quotas.

PA-ODR is preference-aware only on the reserve side: the open category is filled by raw score regardless of preferences, so a student who strictly prefers a reserve category to $o$ may nonetheless be placed in $o$. Section~\ref{sec:paodr-limits} shows that this is not a cosmetic feature; it is what costs PA-ODR both freedom from justified envy and strategy-proofness.

\subsubsection{What triggers de-reservation.}
\label{sec:trigger}
It is worth being precise about the stopping rule, because it is stronger than score-feasibility alone requires. PA-ODR halts when $|\mu_t|=\hat q^t$ for every $t$, that is, when the outcome is reserve-complete with respect to the \emph{current} effective capacities. So the mechanism de-reserves whenever a reserve seat cannot be filled, not merely when the score rule cannot otherwise be met. The two are not the same, and the difference is real rather than notional.

Consider again the instance of Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf}, with $q=(1,1,1)$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$ and students $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(82,\{t_2\})$. In the first iteration $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $e=100$, admissibility requires $\sigma(i)\ge85$, and only $i_2$ qualifies, so $G(100)$ has maximum matching size $1$ and one reserve category is left short. PA-ODR therefore de-reserves, ending at $\hat q=(2,0,1)$ with $\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2\}$ and $\mu_{t_2}=\{i_3\}$. Yet the matching $\nu$ of that proposition---$\nu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\nu_{t_2}=\{i_2\}$, $\nu_{t_1}=\emptyset$---is score-feasible, non-wasteful and within-category fair \emph{under the original capacities}. Score-feasibility alone did not require destroying a reserve seat; leaving one idle would have done.

The design choice is deliberate but should be argued for rather than assumed. Its defence is coverage: on this instance PA-ODR matches all three students while $\nu$ matches only two, and de-reservation is what converts a seat that no eligible student can occupy into a seat that some student can. Its cost is that a stated quota is retired rather than merely left unmet in the current round, which is irreversible in a way that an idle seat is not. A variant that leaves unfillable reserve capacity idle---halting as soon as no further edge can be added rather than insisting on reserve-completeness---is well defined and inherits score-feasibility by the same argument, but it forfeits that coverage gain, and we do not pursue it here. Readers should keep in mind that the results below, and the objective of \textsc{MinDeres} in Section~\ref{sec:order}, are all stated relative to the reserve-completeness stopping rule.

The following monotonicity property is the structural reason the loop behaves well, and we use it below.

\begin{lemma}[The open cutoff falls monotonically]
\label{lem:monotone}
Let $e^{(1)},e^{(2)},\dots$ be the open cutoffs computed in successive iterations of PA-ODR. Then $e^{(1)}\ge e^{(2)}\ge\cdots$, and the open assignments are nested, $\mu_o^{(1)}\subseteq\mu_o^{(2)}\subseteq\cdots$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each non-terminating iteration increases $\hat q^o$ by exactly one and leaves $\sigma$ unchanged. The set $\mu_o$ is always the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ students by raw score, and these sets are nested in $\hat q^o$ because raw scores are distinct. The cutoff $e$ is the minimum raw score over a weakly larger set, hence weakly smaller.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:monotone} identifies the two opposing forces that make de-reservation delicate. Lowering $e$ relaxes the admissibility requirement $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$, so edges are only ever added to $G(e)$ as the algorithm proceeds; but the same step moves one more student from the reserve pool into $\mu_o$, so left vertices are only ever removed. Neither effect dominates in general, which is precisely why a single de-reservation can fail to help, as the second branch of Example~\ref{ex:order} shows.

\subsection{Order Dependence and Non-Minimality}
\label{sec:order}

De-reservation can make otherwise infeasible reserve assignment possible, but it also destroys reserve capacity. A natural normative requirement is therefore to de-reserve as few seats as possible. PA-ODR does not meet this requirement because each de-reservation decision changes both the open cutoff and the pool of students left for reserve assignment.

\begin{example}[The order $\prec$ changes how many seats are de-reserved]
\label{ex:order}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$ with
\[
q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1,\qquad k=5,\qquad \delta=10,
\]
and consider three students
\[
i_1(100,\emptyset),\qquad i_2(84,\{t_1\}),\qquad i_3(83,\{t_2\}).
\]
In the first iteration $\hat q=(1,1,1)$, so $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $e=100$. Reserve admissibility requires $\sigma(i)+5\ge 90$, and neither $84+5=89$ nor $83+5=88$ clears the bar, so $G(100)$ has no edges and both $t_1$ and $t_2$ are unfilled. The two possible choices of $t^\ast$ now diverge.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{If $t_1\prec t_2$}, the algorithm de-reserves $t_1$, giving $\hat q=(2,0,1)$. Then $\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2\}$ and $e=84$, so admissibility requires $\sigma(i)+5\ge 74$. The remaining student $i_3$ satisfies $83+5=88\ge 74$, so the edge $(i_3,t_2)$ is present and $i_3$ is assigned to $t_2$. Every effective capacity is now met and the algorithm returns
\[
\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2\},\qquad \mu_{t_2}=\{i_3\},
\]
having de-reserved a single seat.

\smallskip
\noindent\emph{If $t_2\prec t_1$}, the algorithm de-reserves $t_2$, giving $\hat q=(2,1,0)$. Then $\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2\}$ and $e=84$ as before, but $i_2$ has been absorbed into the open category and she was the only student eligible for $t_1$. Hence $G(84)$ contains no edge into $t_1$, and $|\mu_{t_1}|=0<1=\hat q^{t_1}$ triggers a second de-reservation, giving $\hat q=(3,0,0)$. The algorithm then returns
\[
\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2,i_3\},
\]
having de-reserved two seats.
\end{example}

Both runs match all three students, so the difference is not one of coverage. It is institutional: under one order a reserve quota survives and $i_3$ is admitted through $t_2$, while under the other both reserve quotas are destroyed and $i_3$ enters through the open category. Since the whole purpose of a reserve system is to admit some students through the reserve route, this is a substantive difference, and Example~\ref{ex:order} shows that PA-ODR does not minimize the number of de-reserved seats.

Unlike the phenomena of Section~\ref{sec:spse}, this one is not overlap-driven, and we do not present it as such: no student in Example~\ref{ex:order} is eligible for two reserve categories. The mechanism at work is the feedback between de-reservation and the open cutoff isolated in Lemma~\ref{lem:monotone}---de-reserving $t_2$ pulls $i_2$, the only student eligible for $t_1$, into the open category and thereby empties $t_1$---and it would arise in a single-eligibility model as well. We record it because it is a property of the mechanism we propose, not because it is a further consequence of overlap. Overlap makes the phenomenon worse rather than causing it, since an overlapping student absorbed into the open category can empty several reserve categories at once.

We therefore state the properties of PA-ODR relative to the capacity vector it actually produces. The minimization problem itself, however, need not be left entirely open. Say that an effective capacity vector $\hat q$ with $\hat q^t\le q^t$ for all $t$ and $\hat q^o=q^o+\sum_{t\in T}(q^t-\hat q^t)$ is \emph{realizable} if, on filling the open category with the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ raw scores and inducing the graph $G(e)$ from the resulting cutoff, $G(e)$ admits a capacitated matching saturating $\hat q^t$ for every $t$. \textsc{MinDeres} is the problem of finding a realizable $\hat q$ minimizing the total de-reservation $\sum_{t\in T}(q^t-\hat q^t)$. It is always solvable, since $\hat q^t=0$ for all $t$ is vacuously realizable. Note that realizability, like PA-ODR's stopping rule, demands that every surviving quota be filled exactly; the discussion in Section~\ref{sec:trigger} of what that rules out applies verbatim here.

The following observation is a brute-force enumeration and we claim nothing more for it than that it settles the bounded-$|T|$ case; its interest is in what it says about PA-ODR rather than in the algorithm itself.

\begin{proposition}[Minimum de-reservation is tractable for a fixed number of categories]
\label{prop:minderes}
\textsc{MinDeres} is solvable in time $O\bigl(\prod_{t\in T}(q^t+1)\cdot \Phi\bigr)$, where $\Phi$ is the cost of one capacitated bipartite matching computation. In particular it is solvable in polynomial time whenever $|T|$ is bounded by a constant.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A candidate vector is determined by the numbers $q^t-\hat q^t\in\{0,1,\dots,q^t\}$, so there are exactly $\prod_{t\in T}(q^t+1)$ candidates, and $\hat q^o$ is determined by the choice. For a fixed candidate the open assignment, and hence the cutoff $e$ and the graph $G(e)$, are determined; realizability is then the question of whether $G(e)$ admits a capacitated matching saturating every $\hat q^t$, which is a single maximum-cardinality computation. Enumerating all candidates and returning a realizable one of minimum total de-reservation is therefore correct, and the running time is as claimed. Since $q^t\le|I|$ for every category that can ever be filled, the number of candidates is at most $(|I|+1)^{|T|}$, which is polynomial in the input for fixed $|T|$.
\end{proof}

The construction is an explicit exponential in $|T|$ and we do not claim it is practical for many categories; whether \textsc{MinDeres} is fixed-parameter tractable in $|T|$, or NP-hard when $|T|$ is part of the input, remains open. What Proposition~\ref{prop:minderes} does establish is that the order dependence of PA-ODR is a property of the greedy schedule, not an intrinsic intractability of the objective.

\subsection{Properties of PA-ODR}
The important qualifier in what follows is ``with respect to the final capacity vector.'' Once de-reservation is part of the model, feasibility and non-wastefulness should be judged against the final vector $\hat q$ produced by the algorithm, rather than only against the initial vector $q$. In light of Example~\ref{ex:order}, this qualifier is a genuine weakening and not merely a bookkeeping convention.

\begin{definition}[Non-wastefulness relative to the final effective capacities]
\label{def:nw-hat}
Let $\hat q=(\hat q^o,(\hat q^t)_{t\in T})$ be the final effective capacity vector produced by PA-ODR. A matching $\mu$ is non-wasteful relative to $\hat q$ if there do not exist an unmatched student $i$ and a category $c\in V_i$ such that adding $i$ to category $c$ yields a matching that is feasible with respect to $\hat q$ and score-feasible.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Termination]
PA-ODR terminates after finitely many iterations.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Whenever PA-ODR does not stop, it selects some reserve category $t^\ast$ with $|\mu_{t^\ast}|<\hat q^{t^\ast}$ and updates
\[
\hat q^{t^\ast}\gets \hat q^{t^\ast}-1,\qquad \hat q^o\gets \hat q^o+1.
\]
Hence the total effective reserve capacity
\[
\sum_{t\in T}\hat q^t
\]
decreases by exactly one at every non-terminating iteration. This quantity is a nonnegative integer and is initially $\sum_{t\in T}q^t$, so after at most $\sum_{t\in T}q^t$ such decreases the algorithm must stop.
\end{proof}

Termination alone does not give a polynomial running time, because $\sum_{t\in T}q^t$ is exponential in the input size if capacities are encoded in binary. The gap is closed by a preprocessing step rather than by a better argument.

\begin{nremark}[Running time]
\label{rem:paodr-time}
Replace each $q^c$ by $\min\{q^c,|I|\}$ before running PA-ODR. This changes no output, since a category can never hold more than $|I|$ students and both the open assignment and the capacitated matching are unaffected by capacity above $|I|$; it is also the normalization under which no seat is de-reserved that could not have been filled by any student whatsoever. After it, $\sum_{t\in T}\hat q^t\le|I|\,|T|$, so PA-ODR performs $O(|I|\,|T|)$ iterations. Each iteration sorts by raw score, builds $G(e)$ and solves one instance of \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}, which is polynomial by Remark~\ref{rem:computation}. Hence PA-ODR runs in time polynomial in the input size.
\end{nremark}

\begin{proposition}[Feasibility with respect to the final capacity vector]
The output of PA-ODR is feasible with respect to the final effective capacities $(\hat q^o,(\hat q^t)_{t\in T})$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
In every iteration, the open category is assigned at most $\hat q^o$ students, namely the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ raw-score students. Reserve assignments are computed on the set of students not assigned to the open category, and each reserve category $t$ is given capacity $\hat q^t$ in the bipartite matching problem. Edges of $G(e)$ only join $i$ to categories $t\in\T(i)$, so eligibility holds. Therefore no student is assigned twice and no category exceeds its current effective capacity. At termination, the returned assignment is exactly the assignment from the final iteration, so it is feasible with respect to the final effective capacities.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Score-feasibility]
The output of PA-ODR is score-feasible.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix the final iteration and let $e$ be its open cutoff, so that $e_o(\mu)=e$. By construction, a reserve edge $(i,t)$ is present in $G(e)$ only if
\[
\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta.
\]
Every student assigned to reserve category $t$ in the final matching is chosen along such an edge. Therefore, for every nonempty reserve category $t$,
\[
e_t^+(\mu)=\min_{i\in\mu_t}\sigma_t(i)\ge e-\delta=e_o(\mu)-\delta.
\]
Rearranging gives $e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le \delta$. For empty reserve categories the condition is vacuous by Definition~\ref{def:scorefeas}. Hence the final matching is score-feasible.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Non-wastefulness with respect to the final capacity vector]
The output of PA-ODR is non-wasteful with respect to the final effective capacities.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mu$ be the final output under the final effective capacities $\hat q$. First consider the open category. By construction, $\mu_o$ contains the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ raw-score students, so either every student is matched or $|\mu_o|=\hat q^o$; in the latter case no unmatched student can be added to $o$ without exceeding $\hat q^o$.

Next consider a reserve category $t$. At termination, $|\mu_t|=\hat q^t$ for every $t$; otherwise the stopping condition would have failed and the algorithm would have performed another de-reservation step. Thus no unmatched student can be added to $t$ while respecting the final effective capacities. Therefore no addition of an unmatched student yields a matching that is feasible with respect to $\hat q$, and a fortiori none yields a matching that is both feasible with respect to $\hat q$ and score-feasible.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Within-category fairness]
\label{prop:paodr-fair}
The output of PA-ODR is fair within categories in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:fair}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix the final iteration, let $e$ be its open cutoff and let $\mu$ be the output. Suppose, for contradiction, that there are $t\in T$ and students $i,j$ with $t\in\T(i)\cap\T(j)$, $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$, $j\in\mu_t$ and $i\notin M(\mu)$.

We first check that $(i,t)$ is an edge of $G(e)$. Since $i\notin M(\mu)$ we have in particular $i\notin\mu_o$, so $i$ appears on the left side of $G(e)$. Since $j\in\mu_t$, the pair $(j,t)$ is an edge of $G(e)$, whence $\sigma_t(j)=\sigma(j)+k\ge e-\delta$. Combining this with $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$ gives $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$, and $t\in\T(i)$ holds by assumption, so $(i,t)$ is an edge as claimed. This is the crucial step: a student who outranks a matched student inside a category is automatically admissible at the prevailing cutoff, so the cutoff never shields a matched student from a stronger claimant.

Now replace $j$ by $i$ in $\mu_t$. The result is a capacitated matching on $G(e)$ of the same cardinality, because $(i,t)$ is an edge and $i$ was matched to nothing. Its multiset of matched elevated scores is obtained from that of $\mu$ by replacing $\sigma_t(j)$ with the strictly larger $\sigma_t(i)$, so its weakly decreasingly sorted score vector is lexicographically strictly larger. This contradicts the choice of $\mu$ under criteria 1 and 2 of \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}.
\end{proof}

It is worth being explicit about what this does and does not say, since the qualifier ``with respect to the final capacity vector'' attaches to the two previous propositions but not to this one. PA-ODR satisfies the same within-category fairness notion as SPSE, unweakened, and in particular no separate de-reservation-adjusted version is needed. The reason is the combination of Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf} and the first step of the proof above: an upward within-category claim can neither break score-feasibility nor be blocked by the cutoff. Unlike SPSE, however, the property does not come from rejection monotonicity; it follows from the optimality of the chosen reserve matching inside the final feasibility graph, once de-reservation has fixed the effective capacities and the open cutoff.

\subsection{What PA-ODR Gives Up}
\label{sec:paodr-limits}

Enforcing score-feasibility is not free, and the price is paid on the incentive and envy side rather than on the fairness side. Both failures have the same source, namely that PA-ODR fills the open category by raw score without consulting preferences, and then lets preferences matter only as a tie-breaker.

\begin{example}[PA-ODR admits justified envy]
\label{ex:paodr-envy}
Let $T=\{t_1\}$ with $q^o=q^{t_1}=1$, $k=5$ and $\delta=10$, and take two students
\[
i_1(100,\{t_1\}),\qquad i_2(90,\{t_1\}),
\]
both of whom rank $t_1$ above $o$. PA-ODR assigns the top raw score to the open category, so $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $e=100$. The remaining student $i_2$ satisfies $90+5=95\ge 100-10$, so $(i_2,t_1)$ is an edge and $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$; every effective capacity is met and the algorithm stops without de-reserving. The outcome is score-feasible, since $e_o(\mu)-e_{t_1}^+(\mu)=100-95=5\le\delta$.

Yet $i_1$ is assigned to $o$ while she strictly prefers $t_1$, and the student who occupies $t_1$ satisfies $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=95<105=\sigma_{t_1}(i_1)$. So $i_1$ has justified envy toward $i_2$ and Definition~\ref{def:nje} fails.

The failure is not one that the score rule excuses, which matters because Definition~\ref{def:nje} was deliberately left unweakened. Honouring $i_1$'s claim yields $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_1\}$ and $\mu_o=\{i_2\}$, with $e_o=90$ and $e_{t_1}^+=105$, hence a gap of $-15\le\delta$: the alternative is score-feasible, so the envy survives the score-relative reading of Definition~\ref{def:nje} discussed in Section~\ref{sec:model}. It is also exactly what SPSE returns on this instance, both students applying to $t_1$, which retains $i_1$ and rejects $i_2$, who is then held by $o$. The two mechanisms therefore differ here purely in whose claim they honour, with no feasibility excuse available to either.
\end{example}

\begin{proposition}[PA-ODR is not strategy-proof]
\label{prop:paodr-notsp}
There is an instance in which a student obtains a strictly $\succ_i$-better assignment under PA-ODR by misreporting her category preference.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$ with
\[
q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1,\qquad k=5,\qquad \delta=10,
\]
and take three students
\[
i_1(100,\emptyset),\qquad i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\}),\qquad i_3(94,\{t_1,t_2\}),
\]
with true preferences $t_1\succ_{i_2}t_2\succ_{i_2}o$ and $o\succ_{i_3}t_1\succ_{i_3}t_2$. In every run below, $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $e=100$, and both $i_2$ and $i_3$ clear the admissibility bar, since $95+5=100\ge 90$ and $94+5=99\ge 90$. Hence $G(100)$ contains all four edges between $\{i_2,i_3\}$ and $\{t_1,t_2\}$, the maximum cardinality is $2$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:matroid} both students are matched under any run. Criterion 2 is therefore silent and criterion 3 decides, comparing only the two assignments
\[
\alpha:\ i_2\mapsto t_1,\ i_3\mapsto t_2,
\qquad
\beta:\ i_2\mapsto t_2,\ i_3\mapsto t_1 .
\]

Under truthful reporting the relevant ranks are $r_{i_2}(t_1)=1$, $r_{i_2}(t_2)=2$, $r_{i_3}(t_1)=2$ and $r_{i_3}(t_2)=3$. The decreasingly sorted rank vectors are $(3,1)$ for $\alpha$ and $(2,2)$ for $\beta$, and $(2,2)$ is lexicographically smaller, so criterion 3 selects $\beta$ and $i_2$ is assigned to $t_2$.

Now let $i_2$ report $\hat\succ_{i_2}$ given by $t_1\succ o\succ t_2$, demoting $t_2$ below the open category while leaving $t_1$ on top. The reported ranks become $\hat r_{i_2}(t_1)=1$ and $\hat r_{i_2}(t_2)=3$, so the sorted vectors are $(3,1)$ for $\alpha$ and $(3,2)$ for $\beta$. They agree in the first coordinate, and $1<2$ in the second, so criterion 3 now selects $\alpha$ and $i_2$ is assigned to $t_1$. Since $t_1\succ_{i_2}t_2$ truly, the misreport makes $i_2$ strictly better off.
\end{proof}

The manipulation is the one any egalitarian tie-breaker invites: by reporting a category as worse than it is, a student makes every assignment that gives it to her look worse for her, which under a min--max rule is worth doing whenever she is the one who would otherwise be sacrificed. Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-notsp} is not an artifact of criterion 3 in particular; it reflects the fact that PA-ODR is not a deferred-acceptance mechanism, and by Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} it cannot simply be replaced by one that keeps the capacities fixed.

\section{Conclusion}
Overlapping reserve categories create a genuine allocation problem because admitted students must be counted in exactly one category. Score constraints make feasibility depend on endogenous cutoffs, so non-wastefulness must be read relative to the score rule, and full capacity utilization can fail to exist even when every quota is individually honourable. Within-category fairness, by contrast, survives unchanged, for the reason recorded in Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf}; no justified envy sits between the two, in that a score-relative weakening of it would have content, though none of our results turn on which reading is taken.

We studied two mechanisms. SPSE combines common reserve-side score elevation with deferred-acceptance logic and keeps the initial reserve capacities fixed, and it inherits the full incentive and no-envy properties of deferred acceptance, but it need not satisfy score-feasibility. PA-ODR enforces the score constraint by filling the open category first and converting unfulfillable reserve capacity into open capacity when necessary; it retains within-category fairness in the unweakened sense, but it loses both strategy-proofness and freedom from justified envy, and it retires reserve seats in circumstances where an idle seat would also have sufficed. Table~\ref{tab:comparison} summarizes the comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Comparison of the two mechanisms. Above the rule both mechanisms satisfy the property, although PA-ODR does so only relative to the effective capacities it produces; below the rule they differ outright.}
\label{tab:comparison}
\centering
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{SPSE} & \textbf{PA-ODR} \\
\midrule
One-to-one counting & Yes & Yes \\
Feasibility & Yes & Yes, w.r.t.\ final $\hat q$ \\
Non-wastefulness & Yes & Yes, w.r.t.\ final $\hat q$ \\
Within-category fairness (Def.~\ref{def:fair}) & Yes & Yes \\
\midrule
Score-feasibility & Not guaranteed & Enforced by construction \\
No justified envy (Def.~\ref{def:nje}) & Yes & No (Ex.~\ref{ex:paodr-envy}) \\
Strategy-proofness & Yes & No (Prop.~\ref{prop:paodr-notsp}) \\
Capacity reallocation & Fixed at $q$ & Endogenous, not minimal \\
Uses category preferences & Through proposals & Through lexicographic tie-breaking \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The main design tension is therefore between fixed reserve capacities and score-feasibility. Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} shows that feasibility, non-wastefulness, and within-category fairness do not imply score-feasibility, while Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} shows that full open and reserve utilization can be impossible under the cutoff rule even when every quota is individually honourable. PA-ODR restores score-feasibility by allowing capacity reallocation, but Example~\ref{ex:order} shows that this reallocation is order-dependent and need not minimize de-reservation. The table makes the shape of the trade-off explicit: score-feasibility is bought with the incentive and envy properties of deferred acceptance, and by Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} it cannot be bought merely by choosing a better mechanism with the capacities held fixed.

We should not overstate what is attributable to overlap. That a score-elevated mechanism can breach a cutoff-gap rule, and that a de-reservation schedule can be order-dependent, are both already visible without it, as Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause} and the discussion after Example~\ref{ex:order} record. The specifically overlapping content of the paper is narrower and, we think, sharper: the routing of a multiply eligible student is an allocation decision that can by itself decide score-feasibility, and the joint-versus-individual gap of Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} is a Hall-type obstruction (Remark~\ref{rem:hall}) that vanishes without overlap.

Several questions remain. One is to characterize the instances on which SPSE is automatically score-feasible; Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} shows the class is proper, but not where its boundary lies, and by Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause} such a characterization must rule out two independent causes of failure at once. A second is whether the incentive loss of Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-notsp} is unavoidable: is there any score-feasible and strategy-proof mechanism in this environment, necessarily de-reserving, and if so at what cost in reserve capacity? A third is the complexity of \textsc{MinDeres} when $|T|$ is part of the input, for which Proposition~\ref{prop:minderes} gives only the bounded case; we conjecture hardness, since the joint-fillability condition underlying realizability is a Hall-type condition over all subsets of $T$. A fourth is to compare PA-ODR against the idle-seat variant of Section~\ref{sec:trigger} on the dimensions a policymaker would actually weigh, namely reserve seats retained versus students matched. Finally, the model deliberately assumes a common bonus $k$; allowing category-specific bonuses would break the property that an overlapping student is admissible either for all of her categories or for none, which is what keeps the feasibility graph as simple as it is here.
